WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Raw pointers should not silently transfer ownership.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:11.880
If a function returns a raw pointer, callers may not know whether they must delete it, borrow it briefly, or treat it as an external handle.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.780
That uncertainty creates leaks, double cleanup, and stale access.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.400
Modern C++ designs should make ownership transfer visible through types and conventions.

5
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.600
std::unique_ptr is usually the clearest choice when one owner controls a heap object's lifetime.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.420
A factory returning unique_ptr tells the caller that ownership has been handed off.

7
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.520
A function accepting unique_ptr by value tells reviewers that the callee takes ownership.

8
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:48.580
std::shared_ptr should be reserved for intentional shared lifetime, where multiple owners genuinely participate in keeping the object alive.

9
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:55.480
std::weak_ptr is useful for observing shared objects without extending their lifetime.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.560
Raw pointers and references remain useful for non-owning access.

11
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.780
The important rule is that they must not imply ownership.

12
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:13.720
The .get() member of a smart pointer can be appropriate for temporary borrowed access during a call, but the callee should not store that pointer unless another contract guarantees lifetime.

13
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:20.380
The .release() member is review-sensitive because it gives up ownership without deleting the object.

14
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.000
A clear new owner must take responsibility immediately.

15
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.560
Good boundaries keep ownership and borrowing separate.

16
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:37.100
Factory return values, member fields, handoff functions, and interop wrappers should all answer the same question: who owns this object now?

17
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.720
When interoperating with older APIs, wrap ownership transitions at the narrowest boundary.

18
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:53.860
Convert raw factory results immediately into smart pointers or RAII types, and pass borrowed raw pointers only for the duration of the call so the contract is visible.

19
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.880
If that answer is not obvious, the pointer design needs more work before the code is safe to maintain.
